<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tees</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tees is a river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, flowing about 137 kilometres east from Cross Fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at Teesside. For much of its length it formed the historic boundary between County Durham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the frontier between Bernicia and Deira, and later the southern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' palatinate of Durham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tees.jpg" alt="River Tees" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nehpetsrob · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:High_Force_waterfall_showing_twin_falls._Teesdale,_England.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tees rises at Teeshead, just below Cross Fell, at an elevation of about 732 metres, and drains some 1,800 square kilometres. Its tributaries include the Greta, Lune, Balder, Leven and Skerne. Below the Cow Green Reservoir, built to store water for the industries of Teesside, it crosses hard basalt and dolerite at Cauldron Snout and drops over the 21-metre High Force and the smaller Low Force.</p><p>It then passes Middleton-in-Teesdale, Barnard Castle, Egglestone Abbey and Rokeby Park, known through Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>'s poem Rokeby, before meandering across the plain by Darlington, Yarm, Stockton-on-Tees and Middlesbrough. In its highest reach it divided the historic counties of Westmorland and Durham. The name is possibly of Brittonic origin, perhaps from a word meaning warmth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> the lower river wandered in wide loops between Stockton-on-Tees and Middlesbrough, and a journey by sailing barge from Thornaby to the mouth could take as long as seven days. A proposal of 1769 to cut through the loops led to the Mandale Cut in 1810 and the Portrack Cut in 1831. Later work deepened the channel and made it narrower: ship's ballast and ironworks slag were dumped along the banks, and a river that before 1900 ran in places 300 metres wide now varies between about 100 and 200 metres.</p><p>The river carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from the Durham coalfields, and as ships grew larger the docks at Yarm and Stockton were superseded by deeper ones at Middlesbrough and later at Teesport. That trade drove the growth of Middlesbrough and of the railway between Stockton and Darlington, where George Stephenson developed the locomotive Locomotion; it was the first steam railway to carry passengers, and its shareholders first met in a public house in Yarm. The steelworks of the late 19th century gave the river the nickname The Steel River, and in the 20th century its banks served the chemical industry, notably Imperial Chemical Industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Tees turns tidal below the Tees Barrage, which stands near the site of the Mandale Cut. Teesport lies on reclaimed land on the south side of the estuary below Middlesbrough and is owned by PD Ports. Common seals were absent from the flats at Seal Sands for about a century of heavy industrialisation but have returned, and the area is now the Teesmouth National Nature Reserve. In its upper reaches the valley lies within the North Pennine Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Tees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late 9th century the old kingdom was divided: Viking kings ruled York and the south, while English high-reeves or earls based at Bamburgh governed the north between the <b>Tees</b> and the Tweed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops had their own courts, chancery, mint, sheriff and army, and royal writs did not normally run between the Tyne and the <b>Tees</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-durham">County Palatine of Durham</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Great Heathen Army took York in 867, the northern part of the Kingdom of Northumbria between the <b>Tees</b> and the Tweed remained under English rulers based at Bamburgh.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-bamburgh">Earldom of Bamburgh</a></li><li><blockquote>Its people were Angles who settled the coast between the <b>Tees</b> and the Forth among the British population.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Heathen Army captured York in 866 to 867, and in 876 Halfdan shared out the land of Northumbria south of the <b>Tees</b> among his followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-york">Kingdom of York</a></li></ul></section>
